                    "title": "Bài 1 · History Taking in Clinical Practice",
                    "instruction": "Read the clinical text and answer the questions.",
                    "instruction_vi": "",
                    "passage": "Taking a comprehensive medical history is often considered the most critical component of clinical diagnosis. A structured history begins with the patient's chief complaint—the primary reason for seeking medical attention. \n\nThe physician then explores the History of Present Illness (HPI) using systematically structured frameworks like the SOCRATES mnemonic (Site, Onset, Character, Radiation, Associations, Time course, Exacerbating/relieving factors, and Severity). Physical examination, including palpation and auscultation, follows history taking to validate initial diagnostic hypotheses before ordering invasive tests or imaging.",
